                  LINEAR FORMS IN LOGARITHMS AND APPLICATIONS

This directory formalizes the theory of linear forms in the logarithms of
algebraic numbers created by A. Baker, with its classical applications to
transcendence and to Diophantine equations: the effective lower bound of
Baker and Feldman and the qualitative theorem of Baker (with
Gelfond-Schneider and Hermite-Lindemann as corollaries), Sharpening I of
Baker for algebraic numbers and for algebraic coefficients, explicit bounds
for two logarithms (Laurent), Tijdeman on the Catalan equation, Thue,
Mordell, superelliptic and hyperelliptic equations, integral points on
elliptic curves, the Schinzel-Tijdeman theorem, prime factors of polynomial
values, perfect powers in Lucas sequences, and the imaginary quadratic
fields of class number one and two. It builds on the algebraic number
theory library ANT/.

Load all of it with

  loadt "Baker/make.ml";;

or just one file, together with what it needs, with

  needs "Baker/x.ml";;

Each file starts with needs lines for its prerequisites (library files,
files of ANT/ and of Baker/), followed by prioritize_real();;
prioritize_num();; so that it parses in the same way whatever was loaded
before it. Baker/make.ml first loads the library prerequisites in an order
in which they load together (Library/jacobi.ml before the Multivariate
files, 100/dirichlet.ml before Multivariate/gamma.ml): Library/jacobi.ml,
100/transcendence.ml, Examples/mangoldt.ml, 100/dirichlet.ml,
Multivariate/gamma.ml and Examples/gcdrecurrence.ml; then the 131 files of
the directory in 22 sections. The files of ANT/ load through the needs
lines of the files that use them (57 of the 91), and with them
Library/symmetric_group.ml, galois.ml, ring_groups.ml, modmul_group.ml,
cyclotomic.ml, finite_fields.ml and Multivariate/complex_field.ml;
100/sqrt.ml is needed by Baker/apps.ml. From a fresh hol.ml, loadt
"Baker/make.ml" takes about 70 minutes: about 55 for hol.ml and the library
prerequisites and about 15 for the files of ANT/ and of this directory with
the library files they need.

Three partial loaders load parts of Baker/make.ml:

  Baker/make_core.ml     sections 1 to 4: the Baker core, BAKER_FELDMAN
                         and its applications (about 55 minutes)
  Baker/make_twolog.ml   sections 1 and 9: two logarithms and the Steiner
                         theorem (about 54 minutes)
  Baker/make_laurent.ml  sections 1, 9 and 10: the Laurent bound for two
                         logarithms of rationals (about 54 minutes)

Of the library they need only 100/transcendence.ml, Examples/mangoldt.ml
and the files these load (and 100/sqrt.ml for Baker/apps.ml), which take
almost all of that time.

CONVENTIONS

Algebraic numbers are those of 100/transcendence.ml: algebraic_number z
says that z is a root of a nonzero polynomial with integer coefficients.
Most statements give the polynomials explicitly, as
vsum(0..d) (\j. Cx(a j) * z pow j) = Cx(&0) with integer reals a j and
a d <> 0, with degrees and heights (bounds for the |a j|) as parameters;
Baker/bridge.ml connects the two forms (ALGEBRAIC_NUMBER_EXPLICIT). The
logarithms are any complex numbers lg r with cexp(lg r) algebraic, or the
principal logarithms clog. Linear forms are written
Lambda = beta 0 + sum beta r lg r, and the results give lower bounds for
|Lambda| when Lambda <> 0.

Number fields occur in two forms. The abstract number fields of ANT/
(number_field, ring_of_integers, complex_embeddings, the valuations and
S-units) are used for the subfields of C generated by finitely many
algebraic numbers, subfield_generated complex_ring s
(NUMBER_FIELD_COMPLEX_SUBFIELD_GENERATED). For the Diophantine applications
a number field of degree n is also given by the vector of the n roots of an
irreducible monic integer polynomial, nf_data n c r, and its elements by
their vectors of conjugates (nf_elem, nf_alg_int, nf_order, nf_unit);
Baker/ant_bridge_nf.ml shows that these are the number field Q(r 1) of ANT/,
its ring of integers, the order Z[r 1] and its units, with the n embeddings
nf_embedding n r k.

The Laurent bounds in a number field use nf_proj_height, the average of
log max(|be k|, |ga k|) over the conjugates of an algebraic-integer pair.
This bounds the absolute logarithmic height of be 1 / ga 1 from above
when ga 1 is nonzero; it depends on the chosen pair of coordinates.

The class number of an imaginary quadratic field is the class number of
Gauss, form_classno (qdisc d), the number of reduced primitive binary
quadratic forms of the discriminant; for class number one it is shown to
agree with the ideal class number of ANT/ (Baker/quadint_main.ml).

MAIN THEOREMS

Linear forms in logarithms (Baker, Transcendental Number Theory, Ch. 1-3):

  BAKER_FELDMAN                      Lambda = 0 or |Lambda| >= B^-C (Thm 3.1)
  BAKER_LOWER_BOUND_INDEPENDENT      |Lambda| >= B^-C, Q-independent logarithms
  BAKER_LINEAR_INDEPENDENCE          Thm 2.1: 1 and Q-independent logarithms
                                     are independent over the algebraic numbers
  BAKER_INHOMOGENEOUS                sum beta r lg r is 0 or transcendental
  BAKER_EXP_TRANSCENDENTAL           Thm 2.3, with Hermite-Lindemann
  BAKER_POWER_PRODUCT_TRANSCENDENTAL Thm 2.4
  GELFOND_SCHNEIDER                  a^b transcendental (Hilbert 7th problem)
  EXP_PI_TRANSCENDENTAL              e^pi is transcendental
  SUNIT_GAP                          gaps between S-units (Tijdeman 1973)
  PI_IRRATIONALITY_MEASURE           an irrationality measure for pi
  BAKER_UNIFORM_RATIONAL             a uniform bound for possibly dependent
                                     logarithms of rationals
  PILLAI_FINITE                      a^m - b^n = k <> 0: m and n are bounded
  EXP_PI_SQRT_163_TRANSCENDENTAL     e^(pi sqrt 163) is transcendental

Sharpening I, Tijdeman and the Catalan exponents:

  BAKER_SHARPENING                   Baker 1972 for algebraic numbers:
                                     |Lambda| >= exp(-C g0^K log A log B)
  BAKER_SHARPENING_REAL              the real form
  BAKER_SHARPENING_ALGEBRAIC_COEFFS  algebraic coefficients (Baker 1973):
                                     exp(-C g0^K log A log(B log A))
  SHARPC_SHARPENING_REAL             the real form
  SHARP_BAKER_NUMBER_FIELD           number field form (for Schinzel-Tijdeman)
  TIJDEMAN_LINEAR_FORMS_RATIONAL     Tijdeman 1976, Theorem 2, rationals
  CATALAN_EXPONENT_BOUND             the exponents of x^p - y^q = 1 are bounded

Two logarithms:

  TWOLOG_RATIONAL                    explicit, by interpolation determinants
  TWOLOG_LOG2_LOG3                   |b2 log 2 - b1 log 3| >= exp(-45000 M^2)
  STEINER_CIRCUITS                   the only 3x+1 circuit is 1 -> 2 -> 1
  LAURENT_RATIONAL                   Laurent 2008, Corollary 2, rationals
  LAURENT_RATIONAL_GENERAL           any signs and any positive rationals
  LAURENT_RATIONAL_PARAM             Laurent 2008, Theorem 2, rationals
  LAURENT_NF_REAL                    Corollary 2 in a number field
  LAURENT_NF_REAL_PARAM              Theorem 2 in a number field

The Laurent corollary bounds use 32 in place of 25.2. The parameter bounds
use mu = 1, 4 <= rho <= 8 and h >= 10; in a number field the parameter form
also requires n log 2 / 2 <= h. The number-field forms use the pair heights
described above.

Number fields and Diophantine equations:

  THUE                               Thue equations F(x,y) = m (Thue 1909)
  CUBIC_FORM_REDUCTION               finitely many classes of cubic forms of
                                     given discriminant without integer zero
  MORDELL                            y^2 = x^3 + k (Mordell 1922)
  NF_UNIT_EQUATION                   the unit equation a u + b v = e
  NF_POWER_DESCENT                   the descent d (x - alpha) = xi^m
  SUPERELLIPTIC                      y^m = f(x), m >= 3 (Baker 1969)
  HYPERELLIPTIC                      y^2 = f(x), three simple roots
  SUPERELLIPTIC_SIMPLE_ROOTS         all exponents m >= 2 at once
  ELLIPTIC_INTEGRAL_POINTS           integral points on y^2 = x^3 + a x + b
  WEIERSTRASS_INTEGRAL_POINTS        on the general Weierstrass form
  LEBESGUE                           x^m = y^2 + 1 has no solution, y >= 1
  KO_CHAO_EXPONENT_BOUND             the exponent q of X^2 = Y^q + 1 is bounded
  CATALAN_FINITE                     x^m - y^n = 1 finitely often (Tijdeman)

The Schinzel-Tijdeman theorem (Acta Arith. 31, 1976):

  SCHINZEL_TIJDEMAN                  y^m = f(x), |y| >= 2: m is bounded if f
                                     has two distinct roots
  SCHINZEL_TIJDEMAN_FINITE           finitely many solutions, m >= 3, for two
                                     simple roots
  SCHINZEL_TIJDEMAN_FINITE_SIMPLE_ROOTS
                                     the form with the number of simple roots
  SQUARE_PLUS_POW_FINITE             x^2 + D = y^n, D <> 0, finitely often
  SCHINZEL_TIJDEMAN_REDUCIBLE        the reducible case, as a corollary
  INT_POWER_DESCENT_BOUNDED          the bounded power descent over Z

Prime factors of polynomial values (Polya, Siegel):

  POLY_SMOOTH_VALUES_FINITE          f(x) is S-smooth only finitely often
  POLY_LARGEST_PRIME_FACTOR          greatest prime factor of f(x) -> infinity
  POLY_PRIME_POWER_VALUES_FINITE     f(x) = +-p^n finitely often
  LARGEST_PRIME_FACTOR_SQUARE_PLUS_1 the case n^2 + 1

POLY_LARGEST_PRIME_FACTOR is stated using existence of a large prime
divisor. It also holds for the zero polynomial, since every prime divides
zero. The usual interpretation via the greatest prime factor applies to
nonzero polynomials at nonzero values.

Perfect powers in Lucas sequences (Petho 1982, Shorey-Stewart 1983):

  LUCAS_PERFECT_POWERS_FINITE        u_n = y^m finitely often, Q = +-1
  LUCAS_COMPANION_PERFECT_POWERS_FINITE
                                     the companion sequences v_n
  FIBONACCI_PERFECT_POWERS_FINITE    the Fibonacci numbers
  PELL_PERFECT_POWERS_FINITE         the Pell numbers
  LUCAS_NUMBERS_PERFECT_POWERS_FINITE
                                     the Lucas numbers
  POW_PLUS_POW_FINITE                x^k + D = y^n for fixed k >= 2
  SQUARE_PLUS_MUL_POW_FINITE         x^2 + C = D y^n, n >= 3

The class number problems (Baker, Transcendental Number Theory, Ch. 5):

  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE                   the squarefree d of class number one are
                                     1, 2, 3, 7, 11, 19, 43, 67 and 163
                                     (Baker 1966, Stark 1967, Heegner 1952)
  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE_BOUND_LAURENT     d < 10^16 by the Laurent bound
  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE_FINITE            finiteness (also by BAKER_FELDMAN)
  CLASS_NUMBER_TWO_FINITE            only finitely many d of class number two
                                     (Baker 1971, Stark 1971)
  CLASS_NUMBER_TWO_EXAMPLES          the eighteen known d

Unique factorization in imaginary quadratic fields and its applications:

  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE_UFD               the ring of integers of Q(sqrt(-d)) is a
                                     UFD iff d = m^2 k, k one of the nine
  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE_PID               the same for PID
  CLASS_NUMBER_ONE_UNIQUE_FACTORIZATION
                                     unique factorization into irreducibles
  IMAGINARY_QUADRATIC_CLASS_NUMBER_ONE
                                     abstract fields of degree 2: discriminant
                                     -3, -4, -7, -8, -11, -19, -43, -67, -163
  IMAG_QUADRATIC_INTEGERS_UFD        Euclidean rings for d = 1, 2, 3, 7, 11
  RAMANUJAN_NAGELL                   x^2 + 7 = 2^n (Nagell 1948)
  FERMAT_26                          only 26 lies between a square and a cube
  FLT3                               Fermat Last Theorem for the exponent 3

DEFINITIONS

Every constant defined in this directory, one line each, by topic:

Algebraic integers and polynomials (Baker/algint.ml, intpoly.ml):
  algebraic_integer         a root of a monic integer polynomial
  int_polyfun               integer polynomial functions of degree <= n
  intpoly                   integer polynomials with degree and length bounds

The Baker core (Baker/delta.ml, auxiliary.ml, parametric_core.ml,
estimates.ml, param.ml, explicit.ml, uniform.ml):
  feldman_delta             Delta(z,k) = (z + 1) ... (z + k) / k!
  feldman_basis             the Feldman basis Delta(z,a) Delta(z,h)^(b + 1)
  baker_index               the index set of the auxiliary function
  baker_gamma               its coefficients mu r + mu n beta r
  baker_omega               its exponent mu n beta 0 + sum gamma_r lg r
  baker_term                its terms
  baker_aux                 the auxiliary function F_m of Baker
  baker_algterm             the terms of the algebraic counterpart
  baker_alg                 the algebraic counterpart of F_m
  baker_ub                  an upper bound for F_m on a disc
  baker_err                 a bound for F_m minus its counterpart
  baker_pbound              the Siegel bound for the coefficients p
  baker_nmax                a bound for a common multiple of l + 1, ..., l + h
  baker_liou5               the Liouville lower bound of Lemma 5
  baker_liou7               the Liouville lower bound of Lemma 7
  baker_vb                  the gap of the dichotomy of Lemma 6
  baker_lu                  an explicit exponent bounding baker_ub
  baker_lw                  the explicit exponent for Lemma 5
  baker_l7                  the explicit exponent for Lemma 7
  baker_a                   the parameter a = 8 D + 8
  baker_c                   the parameter c = 8 D + 10
  baker_kpar                the parameter k = q^(c (n + 1))
  baker_Lpar                the parameter L = 2 D q^(c n)
  baker_J                   the parameter J = c (2 n^2 + 2 n) + 2
  baker_e1                  the exponent e1 = c (n + 1) + J + 4
  baker_qreq                an explicit lower bound for the base q
  baker_cexp                an explicit lower bound for the exponent C
  baker_logdata             size data of a family of logarithms

Sharpening I (Baker/sharp_defs.ml, uniform.ml, catalan.ml):
  sharp_index               the index set of the auxiliary function
  sharp_x                   its nodes bb n mu r - bb r mu n
  sharp_omega               its exponent sum (x_r / bb n) lg r
  sharp_term                its terms
  sharp_aux                 the auxiliary function of Sharpening I
  sharp_algterm             the terms of the algebraic counterpart
  sharp_alg                 the algebraic counterpart
  sharp_coeff               the coefficients of the derivative relation
  sharp_coeff0              the coefficient -sum (m i + 1) lg i / bb n
  sharp_algdata             height data of the numbers cexp(lg r)
  sharp_deltarep            integer polynomial forms of the Delta factors
  sharp_psize               the Siegel bound for the coefficients p
  sharp_liou5               the Liouville lower bound at an integer l
  sharp_lb8                 the Liouville lower bound at a rational l / q
  sharp_ubd                 an upper bound for the auxiliary function
  sharp_errb                a bound for its difference with the counterpart
  sharp_lv                  the degree bounds (L for r < n, Ln for r = n)
  sharp_nfbound             bounded conjugates and an integer denominator
  sharp_core_property_alg   the core property, integer coefficients
  sharp_bound_property_alg  the bound property, integer coefficients
  sharpc_coefdata           height data of algebraic coefficients
  sharpc_core_property      the core property, algebraic coefficients
  sharpc_bound_property     the bound property, algebraic coefficients
  sharp_bound_property      the bound property for rationals (Tijdeman)

Two logarithms (Baker/steiner.ml, laurent_defs.ml):
  collatz_t                 the 3x+1 map n -> n / 2 or (3 n + 1) / 2
  circuit                   k odd and l even steps of collatz_t back to n
  laurent_omega             omega of Laurent 2008
  laurent_theta             theta of Laurent 2008
  laurent_C                 the constant C of Laurent 2008, Theorem 2
  laurent_Cp                the constant C prime of Laurent 2008
  laurent_bound             the bound of Laurent 2008, Theorem 2
  laurent_main              the main inequality of the determinant method

Number fields as vectors of conjugates (Baker/nf_defs.ml,
laurent_nf_defs.ml, ant_bridge_nf.ml):
  nf_data                   roots of an irreducible monic integer polynomial
  nf_elem                   the conjugate vectors of the elements of Q(r 1)
  nf_alg_int                those of its algebraic integers
  nf_order                  those of the order Z[r 1]
  nf_unit                   those of the units of Z[r 1]
  nf_embedding              the embedding of Q(r 1) with r 1 -> r k
  nf_proj_height            the archimedean height of a chosen pair

Quadratic forms and fields, class numbers (Baker/classno_defs.ml,
classno_chardefs.ml, cn_defs.ml, quadint_defs.ml):
  qform                     the binary form a x^2 + b x y + c y^2
  qform_rep                 the number of representations of l by a form
  qf_reduced                reduced forms (Lagrange, Gauss)
  form_classno              the class number of Gauss for discriminant D
  qdisc                     the discriminant -d or -4 d of Q(sqrt(-d))
  prin_form                 the principal form x^2 + x y + ((d + 1) DIV 4) y^2
  prin_rep                  its representation numbers
  qchar                     the Jacobi symbol (n | m) as a real number
  kchar4                    the real character (-4 d / m)
  zeta_coprime              the sum of 1 / x^2 over the x coprime to k
  qform_term                the terms chi(Q(x,y)) / Q(x,y)^s of lattice sums
  qform_slice               the sum of qform_term over x, y fixed
  qform_kernel              x (1 + x) / (1 - x)^3, the error kernel
  cyclo_unit                the cyclotomic unit of a real quadratic field
  quad_omega                (1 + sqrt(-d)) / 2 or sqrt(-d)
  imag_quadratic_field      Q(sqrt(-d)) as a set of complex numbers
  imag_quadratic_integers   its ring of integers, a subring of C

Other (Baker/lebesgue.ml, ramanujan_nagell.ml):
  gaussian_integer          the Gaussian integers as complex numbers
  nagell_seq                u 0 = 0, u 1 = 1, u (m + 2) = u (m + 1) - 2 u m

Baker/OVERVIEW.md states the main theorems in full, part by part, and lists
the files by section; the header of every file lists its main results and
the results it uses.

REFERENCES

  A. Baker, "Transcendental Number Theory", Cambridge University Press 1975.
  A. Baker, "A sharpening of the bounds for linear forms in logarithms I",
  Acta Arith. 21 (1972).
  A. Baker and G. Wustholz, "Logarithmic Forms and Diophantine Geometry",
  Cambridge University Press 2007.
  Y. Bugeaud, "Linear Forms in Logarithms and Applications", EMS 2018.
  M. Laurent, "Linear forms in two logarithms and interpolation
  determinants II", Acta Arith. 133 (2008).
  R. Tijdeman, "On the equation of Catalan", Acta Arith. 29 (1976).
  A. Schinzel and R. Tijdeman, "On the equation y^m = P(x)", Acta Arith. 31
  (1976).

Baker/OVERVIEW.md lists further references.

